There was a long discussion on how to pick a word for the concept of
'ziltau'. I wish I could remember how we came to the decision.

I would rearrange a bit - tanru are composed of two ziltau, and a
ziltau is either a brivla or cmavo (or a few other constructs.)

I'm also definitely glad you included cmene in there, as I'm fairly
sure none of the existing texts differentiates between cmene and
cmevla.


On Sat, Nov 13, 2010 at 1:58 PM, Pierre Abbat <phma@phma.optus.nu> wrote:
> On Saturday 13 November 2010 04:47:21 Remo Dentato wrote:
>> When talking about lojban grammar we use lojban words that, at first,
>> might be difficult to remember and correlate.
>>
>> I've made a small mindmap to remember them: http://www.dentato.com/map1.png
>>
>> I would be grateful if you would like to have a look and tell me if
>> there's anytihng I missed or got wrong.
>
> A rafsi isn't a tag, it's a bound morpheme. It's called an affix, but properly
> speaking that term applies only to some cmavo rafsi such as "sel".
>
> A sumtcita is an adposition (all sumtcita in Lojban are prepositions). There
> are proper prepositions (all of selma'o BAI) and tense markers, which can
> also be used as prepositions. The word "modal" is misused in the Book; as a
> linguistic term, it means words like "ka'e".
>
> "ziltau" isn't right. "zi'o" deletes one place of a selbri; it doesn't merge
> two together. I suggest "taurpau".
>
